{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00515 - MONDO_3582",
  "verbalization": "Cisplatin, a platinum-based chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hereditary Breast Ovarian Cancer Syndrome through a multi-step mechanism involving the tumor suppressor gene BRCA1. The evidence suggests that Cisplatin may interact with immature ovarian teratomas, which are associated with BRCA1 mutations, and this interaction could lead to the activation of downstream pathways that contribute to cancer development. Rucaparib, a poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor, is indicated in both immature ovarian teratomas and familial ovarian cancer, and its association with Hereditary Breast Ovarian Cancer Syndrome implies a potential role in the disease’s progression. The shared indication of Rucaparib with Hereditary Breast Ovarian Cancer Syndrome further supports a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be elucidated. The evidence from the three paths highlights a potential pathway where Cisplatin, through its interaction with teratomas, may influence the expression of BRCA1-related pathways, thereby contributing to the development of Hereditary Breast Ovarian Cancer Syndrome.",
